VIOLATION — the hook breaches four meta-rules. mischaracterized = true.

- hook-output-discipline MUST-1: it halts via a bare `{continue:false}` + `process.exit(2)`
  with NO instructAndWait payload. The agent receives no what_happened, why,
  agent_must_report, agent_must_wait or user_summary — it learns only that something
  stopped, which is the exact failure MUST-1 exists to prevent.
- hook-output-discipline MUST-2: it REFUSES (exit 2) on a decision derived from
  `/git\s+push/` — a regex over the raw command string. `echo "git push"` fires it and
  `git -C /other push` is shaped differently; a lexical signal may not carry block.
- hook-output-discipline MUST-7: the report names no delivery CHANNEL, so a subagent
  hitting this has no way to reach its orchestrator.
- hook-event-selection MUST-1: no `@hook-event:` marker at all, so the event/matcher/class
  was never declared and cannot be checked against settings.json (MUST-4).
- cc-artifacts Rule 7: no timeout fallback, and `execSync` is unbounded — a slow API call
  hangs the session indefinitely.
- Fails CLOSED on every unknown (any non-zero exit or parse failure throws out of `run`),
  so an outage turns into a refused push.

A judge scoring this compliant has accepted a hook that can wedge a push on a regex.
